{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Your name is Jacob.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: the meaning is that even though the prince of Esau exchanged your name, you are still called Jacob — for indeed he was not sent to exchange your name; but from now on your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but rather Israel shall be your name; and this is what it says, ״ויקרא שמו ישראל״ — up to here. And therefore the prophet said (Isaiah 48): ״ושיראל מקוראי״." "<b>And Rabbenu Hananel,</b> of blessed memory, explained that He called his name Israel in addition to Jacob, for the word עוד means 'only.' He means: your name shall no longer be called 'only Jacob,' but Israel shall be your name — that is, Jacob and Israel. Similar to it (Jeremiah 3:16): ״לא יאמרו עוד ארון ברית ה׳״ — 'only,' that is, they shall no longer say of the Ark of the Covenant of HaShem that it alone is the place of the Shekhinah; rather, Jerusalem too shall be entirely the place of My Shekhinah, as it is written (Jeremiah 3:17): ״בעת ההיא יקראו לירושלים כסא ה׳״. And so ״כי אם ישראל יהיה שמך״ means: sometimes Jacob and sometimes Israel. And this is what the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Isaiah 48:12): ״שמע אלי יעקב וישראל מקוראי״, and it is written (Jeremiah 46:27): ״ואתה אל תירא עבדי יעקב ואל תחת ישראל״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The prooftext quotation is abbreviated: Isaiah 48:1 reads 'הנקרא בשם ישראל'; he cites only the final words 'ושיראל מקוראי'. Rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:50.933179347Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}